-- title:
--   Linked scalars and tame inertia on the exceptional Drinfeld fibre
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and `hι` requires that some ring homomorphism $L \to \mathbb{C}$ carry $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is required by `hH₁` to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$. The intermediate field $K$ between $L$ and the Laurent series field $L((\mathsf q))$ is required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((\mathsf q))$ generated by the image under the coefficientwise map `coeffEmb L` of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a Henselian discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field $\kappa :=$ `ResidueField A`, with $q \in \mathfrak m_A$ (`hAq`) and with $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ has Laurent series `coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq` (the $q$-expansion $\mathsf q^{-1} + \dots$ of the modular $j$-function, transported from $\mathbb{Q}$), and $j \ne 0$. Two elements of $A$ are fixed: a uniformiser $\varpi$ with $\mathfrak m_A = (\varpi)$ (`hϖ`), and an element $\varpi_t$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A (↥K) j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$, and $j_C :=$ `jChartFin A (↥K) j` for $j$ viewed in $C$. A maximal ideal $y$ of $C$ is fixed with $\varpi \in y$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` requires $y$ to be supersingular: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_C)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Let $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A (↥K) j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the scheme obtained as the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ (denoted `XFin`) to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the corresponding algebra for $j^{-1}$ along the middle chart, with structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and canonical morphism `ιFin : XFin ⟶ X`.
--
--   Two global hypotheses about the geometry of $X$ along the fibre over the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ are assumed.
--
--   The first, `hArig` (the rigid Drinfeld chart package at supersingular points), asserts the following for every point $z$ of $X$ at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ (pulled back through `toBase` and the Spec–$\Gamma$ isomorphism) lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal O_z$, every point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin` image $z$, and every $y'$ satisfying the same supersingularity condition as $y$: there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak m_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0, X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv$ [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) $= X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the $\mathfrak m$-adic completion $\widehat{\mathcal O_z}$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f u\bigr)$, such that, writing $\mathrm{toC} : \mathcal O_z \to \widehat{\mathcal O_z}$ for the completion map, $\mathrm{mkS}$ for the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY} : C \to \mathcal O_z$ for the germ map at $z$ through `ιFin`, seven clauses hold: (a) $e$ carries the germ of each $a \in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (b) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` — that is, $\tau$ implements on ratios of $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ the slash action of the matrix `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` — the subalgebra $C$ is $\tau$-stable; (c) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, such a $\tau$ fixes $y'$ pointwise modulo $y'$, i.e. $\tau(a) - a \in y'$ for all $a \in C$; (d) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and fixing $y'$ in this sense, there are $\theta \in \operatorname{Aut}(S)$, $c \in W$ and a $2\times 2$ matrix $M$ over $W$ with: $\theta$ corresponding to $\tau$ under $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$; $\theta$ fixing all constants $C(w)$; $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$; $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij} \pmod{\mathfrak m_W}$; $c \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak m_W}$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$; and $c \not\equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak m_W}$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (e) for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1, P_2$ of $S$, each omitting at least one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and each containing an element $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h_i$ with $h_i \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, with $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$, the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to $\mathcal O_z$ differ; (f) for a prime $P$ of $S$ omitting one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing an element $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ has germ in the contraction of $P$ to $\widehat{\mathcal O_z}$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak m_A$; (g) [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with this element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that the degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \operatorname{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b \in W$ not both in $\mathfrak m_W$ with $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak m_W$, and such that the image of the germ of that element minus $a_0$ equals the class of $h$.
--
--   The second, `hArigI` (the semilinear rider on such charts), asserts: for every $z$, $y'$ as above and every chart datum $(W, \sigma, f, u, v, e)$ as in `hArig`, if clauses (a) and (d) of that package hold for it, then for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, every pair of compatible ring automorphisms $\sigma_L$ of $L$ and $\sigma_A$ of $A$ with $\sigma_A \equiv \mathrm{id} \pmod{\mathfrak m_A}$, every $\pi \in A$ with $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and every $\tilde\alpha$ with $\sigma_A\pi = \tilde\alpha\pi$ and $\tilde\alpha^{q+1} \equiv d \pmod{\mathfrak m_A}$, and every ring automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ acting on Laurent series coefficientwise through $\sigma_L$: $C$ is $\tau$-stable, $\tau$ fixes $y'$ pointwise modulo $y'$, and there exist $\theta \in \operatorname{Aut}(S)$, $\sigma_W \in \operatorname{Aut}(W)$, $\tilde c \in W$ and a matrix $M$ over $W$ with $\theta$ corresponding to $\tau$, $\sigma_W \circ \sigma = \sigma \circ \sigma_A$, $\sigma_W \equiv \mathrm{id} \pmod{\mathfrak m_W}$, $\theta(C(w)) = C(\sigma_W w)$, $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, $\tilde c \equiv \sigma(\tilde\alpha^{q+1})$ and $M_{ij} \equiv \tilde c \cdot \bigl(\mathrm{diag}(1,(d^q)^{-1})\bigr)_{ij} \pmod{\mathfrak m_W}$, the latter matrix being [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.diagOneElem q (d^q)⁻¹`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L233).
--
--   One point $z$ of $X$ in the fibre over the closed point is then fixed together with $\varpi_z$, a point $y'$ of `XFin` above it with the supersingularity condition, and `hy'y : y'.asIdeal = y`; and one chart datum $(W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1)$ at $z$ is fixed, with $W_1$ a complete discrete valuation domain, $\mathfrak m_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$, $u_1, v_1$ units, $f_1 \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$, and $e_1$ an isomorphism of $\widehat{\mathcal O_z}$ with $S := W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$. The hypothesis `hW₁` requires this datum to satisfy all seven clauses (a)–(g) listed above.
--
--   The weighted centre and the blow-up chart are pinned down next. The ideal $J$ of $C$ is required by `hJ` to be the infimum of the set of those ideals $J'$ of $C$ arising as the preimage, under the restriction of some level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, of the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. The subalgebra $B$ of $K$ is required by `hB` to be $C[J/\varpi_t]$: the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K : x\varpi_t \in J\}$, viewed as an $A$-subalgebra. Finally $\mathcal V$ is a valuation subring of $K$ (named `W` in the statement, the identifier being reused) with $B \subseteq \mathcal V$ (`hBW`).
--
--   Four groups of hypotheses describe $B$ and $\mathcal V$. `hR1`: $C \le B$, and every element of $K$ is a quotient $g/h$ with $g, h \in B$, $h \ne 0$. `hR2`: $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation, and $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. `hR3` (five clauses): an element of $L$ lies in $\mathcal V$ exactly when it comes from $A$; $\mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$ is generated by $\varpi$; $\mathcal V$ is a discrete valuation ring; for $b \in C$, $b \in y$ if and only if $b \in \mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$; and $f \in K$ lies in $\mathcal V$ exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$.
--
--   The decomposition block `hEQ` has five clauses. First, for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on $\kappa$ there is a surjection $\rho : B \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) $= \kappa[X_0,X_1]/(\mathrm{drinfeldPoly}_q - 1)$ with $\rho b = 0 \iff b \in \mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$, with $\rho(a) =$ the image of the residue of $a$ for $a \in A$, and such that for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ for which $\mathcal V$ is $\tau$-stable there is $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c) \in$ [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of $(\gamma, c) \mapsto \det(\gamma)\,c^{q+1}$) satisfying $\rho(\tau b) =$ `hAction` $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c)(\rho b)$ for all $b \in B$ with $\tau b \in B$, and with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$. Second, $B$ is stable under every level automorphism attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$. Third, every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached from the exceptional prime: some $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ carry every $b \in B \cap \mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$ into $B$ and into $Q$. Fourth, an element of $B$ all of whose translates by level automorphisms attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ lie in $\mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$ is divisible by $\varpi$. Fifth, a level automorphism attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ (for $b \in C$, $b \in y$ iff $\tau b \in y$) leaves $\mathcal V$ stable.
--
--   Conclusion. For every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on $\kappa$ there exists a ring homomorphism $\rho : B \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) such that:
--
--   (1) $\rho$ is surjective;
--
--   (2) for $b \in B$, $\rho b = 0$ if and only if $b \in \mathfrak m_{\mathcal V}$;
--
--   (3) $\rho(\mathrm{alg}_A(a))$ is the image of the residue class of $a$ under $\kappa \to$ `CoordRing q κ`, for all $a \in A$;
--
--   (4) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` such that $\mathcal V$ is $\tau$-stable, there is $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c) \in$ `hSubgroup q` for which: $\rho(\tau b) =$ `hAction` $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c)(\rho b)$ for all $b \in B$ with $\tau b \in B$; $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$; and — the linked-scalars clause — there exist $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, a level automorphism $\tau'$ at $\gamma'^{-1}$ with $\mathcal V$ stable under $\tau'$, and a proof that $(1, c) \in$ `hSubgroup q`, such that $\rho(\tau' b) =$ `hAction` $(1, c)(\rho b)$ for all $b \in B$ with $\tau' b \in B$; so the same scalar $c$ is realised by a purely scalar element of `hSubgroup q` coming from an element of $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$;
--
--   (5) there exists $\eta \in \{1, q\}$ such that for every $\pi \in A$ with $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$, every pair of compatible ring automorphisms $\sigma_L$ of $L$ and $\sigma_A$ of $A$ (i.e. $\mathrm{alg}_A(\sigma_A a) = \sigma_L(\mathrm{alg}_A a)$) with $\sigma_A \equiv \mathrm{id} \pmod{\mathfrak m_A}$, every $\tilde\alpha \in A$ with $\sigma_A\pi = \tilde\alpha\pi$, every $\alpha \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ whose image in $\kappa$ is the residue of $\tilde\alpha$, and every ring automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ acting on Laurent series coefficientwise through $\sigma_L$: the subalgebra $B$ is $\tau$-stable, $\mathcal V$ is $\tau$-stable ($f \in \mathcal V \iff \tau f \in \mathcal V$), and for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$ whose image in $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ equals $\alpha^{q+1}$, and every proof that $\bigl(\mathrm{diag}(1,(d^\eta)^{-1}), \alpha^\eta\bigr) \in$ `hSubgroup q` — the first coordinate being `diagOneElem q (d^η)⁻¹` — one has $\rho(\tau b) =$ `hAction` $\bigl(\mathrm{diag}(1,(d^\eta)^{-1}), \alpha^\eta\bigr)(\rho b)$ for all $b \in B$ with $\tau b \in B$.
--
--   Thus the conclusion reproduces the first clause of `hEQ` and strengthens it by the linked-scalars clause in (4) and by the tame-inertia law (5) with a single exponent $\eta \in \{1,q\}$ valid for all choices of $\pi$, $\sigma_L$, $\sigma_A$, $\tilde\alpha$, $\alpha$, $\tau$ and $d$.
--
--   This is the step that identifies the exceptional fibre of the weighted blow-up chart at a supersingular point with the Drinfeld curve $\kappa[X_0,X_1]/(\mathrm{drinfeldPoly}_q-1)$ and records, on one and the same reduction map $\rho$, both the action of the level automorphisms with their scalars linked to purely scalar elements of `hSubgroup q` and the tame action of inertia through $(\mathrm{diag}(1,d^{-\eta}),\alpha^{\eta})$; it is the variant for an auxiliary $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-type level with a guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$, needed for small $q$. It feeds the assembly of supersingular affine charts for the integral model of the modular curve at full level.
